WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.700
- I hear you play music by ear.

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:02.730
- Well, it's weird because you know,

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.207
there's this condition called synesthesia

00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:05.040
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.023
- where you can sort of
like your wires are crossed

00:00:06.023 --> 00:00:06.997
- Yeah yeah.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:10.210
- and your sensory pathways
are sort of mixed up.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.380
And so some people can see music.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.300
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.133
- I don't necessarily,

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:17.610
I sort of do that where
whenever I hear a song,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.505
it's almost like these little
Lego shapes in my mind.

00:00:20.505 --> 00:00:21.338
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:22.810
- So that's how I remember arrangement.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.270
That's how I remember composition.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.940
It becomes a visual
thing for me to rely on

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.607
because I can't read music.

00:00:28.607 --> 00:00:29.440
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.273
- I wish I could.

00:00:30.273 --> 00:00:32.500
I mean, I really wish that
I spent more time learning

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.880
more about music, but both
of my parents were musicians.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.690
My father was a classically
trained flautist.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:37.690
My mother sang in singing groups.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.400
- [Kelly] Oh my God it was destiny.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.503
- [Dave] It's kind... I do believe in DNA.

00:00:41.503 --> 00:00:42.336
- Yeah yeah.

00:00:42.336 --> 00:00:44.000
- I really do believe in this
thing that it's passed down.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.070
- And behavioral, you're
around it all the time as well.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.700
- Yeah. And so I just
kinda figured it out.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.220
- Yeah. I gotta be honest with you.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.230
I actually learned singing
and I learned harmony,

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.160
all of that without sheet
music. I was like, you, I,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.750
I could somehow feel it.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.345
Mine was a feeling, like I could feel

00:01:00.345 --> 00:01:01.178
- Right

00:01:01.178 --> 00:01:02.550
- where it was and place it, but my,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.240
I was way better at like hearing it

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.640
and doing it rather than reading.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.473
- Yeah its, uh,

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:07.820
And when I grew up in Virginia

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.630
and my mother was a public school teacher.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.560
- [Kelly] Oh mine was too.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.393
- No way.

00:01:11.393 --> 00:01:14.503
- Yeah, oh our mothers
have talked. That's right.

00:01:14.503 --> 00:01:16.020
Our mothers, because
your mother did the book.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.086
I think, I feel like she...

00:01:17.086 --> 00:01:17.919
- [Dave] Yes.

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:18.752
- ...yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:18.752 --> 00:01:20.494
- My mother wrote a book
called From "Cradle to Stage".

00:01:20.494 --> 00:01:22.160
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah, yeah
- And it was all about...

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:22.993
- Yeah

00:01:22.993 --> 00:01:23.826
I just remembered that.

00:01:23.826 --> 00:01:24.659
- ...mothers and musicians.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:26.325
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, it was really cool.

00:01:26.325 --> 00:01:27.158
- But both of our momma's are teachers.

00:01:27.158 --> 00:01:30.170
- She interviewed like 20
different mothers of musicians.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.440
And, um, what she found were
these really clear parallels,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.840
like most of these people;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.530
Dr. Dre and Pharrell and Miranda Lambert

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.650
and people like that,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.540
they sort of discovered
music in this specific window

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.340
of time in between the
ages of about 10 and 13,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.173
- Yeah

00:01:48.173 --> 00:01:50.630
- Where like identity
and independence sort

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:51.885
of intersect right there.

00:01:51.885 --> 00:01:53.551
- Uh huh.
- And if you're a kid

00:01:53.551 --> 00:01:56.690
and have any sort of musical inclination

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.560
uh, or an instrument falls in your lap,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.390
then most likely you're going
to become a musician because

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.330
that becomes a part of your identity.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.560
Um, and so when I was young,
like I didn't have a drum set,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.167
I would set up pillows on the floor...

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.000
- Yeah.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.833
- ...and just put on my favorite records

00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:11.666
and play along to them.

00:02:11.666 --> 00:02:12.780
- Yeah.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.510
- Um, because I could hear the
difference between like, oh,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.920
that's a kick drum, that's
a snare, that's a symbol.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:18.980
Then it was just a matter of like

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:20.840
the physicality of figuring it out.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.673
- [Kelly] Yeah

00:02:21.673 --> 00:02:22.662
- So

00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:24.570
- [Kelly] That's innovative
- So yeah, it was cool.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.280
Like they didn't have much.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.233
- My instrument was free. So
I just practiced in my room.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.660
- Thank God you did. You know what I mean?

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.920
I started on keys and I
was like, this is not my,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:34.753
this is not my thing.

00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:36.490
And I, I, I loved singing, though,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.310
but I did have my own studio in my closet.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.780
- I took one drum lesson.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.613
- [Kelly] You did?

00:02:40.613 --> 00:02:41.890
- My mother used to take
me to this jazz club

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.630
in Washington, DC on Sundays.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.350
And they had this jazz workshop
thing and there was this

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.850
house drummer, his name is Lenny Robinson.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:52.110
And he was such a bad-ass. I
mean, I was like 15 years old.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.100
Learned to play on the pillows.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.790
And I saw this guy, I'm like,

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.110
I'm going to ask him to give me lessons.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.180
So he's was like 30 bucks an
hour. I'm like, all right.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.440
So he comes over to the
house and I had the drum set

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.450
and he sits down. He does
this crazy jazz solo thing.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:05.992
He goes, let me see what you can do.

00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:07.540
And I'm like ladadada,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.340
and then he goes, okay, first thing,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.591
you're holding your sticks backwards.

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:14.841
(laughter)

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.020
And he's got me on this practice
pad kind of doing this for

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.120
30 bucks an hour. I was like,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.590
I can't afford to become a drummer.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.423
- [Kelly] I can't afford...

00:03:21.423 --> 00:03:22.780
- Like a million dollars.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.340
(Laughter)

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.680
- I was like $30 an hour. Oh my God.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.570
So wait. So your new book, I love this.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:29.793
First, congratulations.

00:03:29.793 --> 00:03:30.626
- Thank you.

00:03:30.626 --> 00:03:31.459
- Number one.

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:32.292
That's amazing.

00:03:32.292 --> 00:03:33.425
And it's called Storyteller.

00:03:33.425 --> 00:03:34.258
- [Dave] Yes.

00:03:34.258 --> 00:03:36.750
- [Kelly] So why did you,
why pick now to write that?

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.250
I feel like everybody in the limelight,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.840
they always want you to write
a book and I'm nervous about

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.807
it, but you.

00:03:41.807 --> 00:03:43.430
- You should do it, because it's fun..

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:45.000
- I love how you've done it
though. You've done it...

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.900
- Well, here's what happened.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.710
So, you know, Fu Fighters
have been a band for 26 years.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:50.625
At this point.

00:03:50.625 --> 00:03:51.997
(cheering)

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:55.537
And like, we spent the last
quarter of a century on tour.

00:03:55.537 --> 00:03:56.370
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.060
So last year, when everything shut down,

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.810
I had nothing to do. And I'm such a spazz.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.379
I was like, what am I going to do?

00:04:02.379 --> 00:04:03.830
I got nothing to do. I can't have nothing.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:04.663
- [Kelly] Where do I put
this creative energy?

00:04:04.663 --> 00:04:07.295
- So you know what I did, I
started an Instagram page.

00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:08.430
(cheering)

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.020
Like my first. Yay,
Instagram. I had no idea.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.760
I was like wait, how's this work?

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.910
And so instead of taking pictures
of like myself in a thong

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.350
in the mirror, I was like,

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.210
how about I'll just write stories.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:22.110
So I started this Instagram
page called Dave's true stories.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.270
And I was writing these little
short stories about like,

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.520
- Pleas tell me this is a gif somewhere.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.060
- That's how I...

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.520
- Jamming with like Bowie,

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:29.659
Jamming with Prince...

00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:30.492
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:04:30.492 --> 00:04:31.325
- ...and all these crazy things that have

00:04:31.325 --> 00:04:33.080
happened in my life.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.020
Once I realized like,
oh, this pandemic thing,

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.320
this might take a while. I'm like, well,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.290
maybe now's time to write a book.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:42.280
So, uh, I had this list
of all these stories

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.210
I was going to write for
the Instagram page and I,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.043
like short stories.

00:04:45.043 --> 00:04:48.650
And I just took that list
and gave it to my editor.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.630
And I said, you pick what
you want me to write.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.570
So she'd say, all right,
write about that car crash

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.000
when you were a kid,
like boom, 5,000 words,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.860
write about, uh, joining
Nirvana, boom, 5,000 words.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.367
So I was writing these short stories.

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:00.200
- [Kelly] Mm.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.940
- It's almost like making a record.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:02.773
So, you know,

00:05:02.773 --> 00:05:04.910
you go into the studio
and you have songs...

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:05.770
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:06.810
- and you start recording

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.480
and then an album starts taking shape.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.610
And then you're like, okay,
this is the first song.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:11.443
This is the last song.

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:12.460
- And then something you
thought was gonna be on there

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:13.293
doesn't make it,

00:05:13.293 --> 00:05:14.330
but then something surprises you.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.670
- Yes, and you you start to
realize like what you have

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:16.870
and what you don't have.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.230
But it was the same way as I
was writing these short stories

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.360
until finally we put it together.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.914
So there was an arc, uh,
from beginning to end.

00:05:24.914 --> 00:05:27.590
And it was really, it
was really rewarding.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.010
Both of my parents were also writers.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.480
My father was a speech
writer and a journalist.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.120
My mother was a creative writing teacher.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.130
So it came naturally, but it was fun.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.540
And you discover a lot about
yourself while you're doing it.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.030
- I know, well, yeah, maybe that's,

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:42.391
I'm not ready for that kind of therapy.

00:05:42.391 --> 00:05:44.060
(laughter)

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.340
- I'm in between therapists right now.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.826
- I'm actually seeing one
and we haven't made it there.

00:05:47.826 --> 00:05:48.970
- Right.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.360
Um, no, it is true though.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.750
I feel like a lot of people
always want to know, you know,

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.440
behind the scenes, but like,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.780
I don't know that you really
do. You know what I'm saying?

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.740
Like some of it. It's like, mm, let's just

00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.573
leave it.

00:05:59.573 --> 00:06:00.970
- Well, you know, think of it this way.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:05.940
It doesn't necessarily have to
be specific or informational

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.270
or, uh, practical or logistic.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:14.050
You can write about like a
much more general emotional

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.310
picture

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:16.143
- [Kelly] Like a broader...

00:06:16.143 --> 00:06:17.983
- of what it's like to be you.
- Yeah.

00:06:17.983 --> 00:06:18.920
- Right. So you don't have to talk about.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.610
- And I don't mean that.
I just mean like the road,

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:21.820
they always want the road too,

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.130
you know, and you're like, no you don't.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.100
- Well, this is,

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.870
this is one of the things
that there were certain,

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.410
there were certain things I,

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.320
I knew what people wanted me to write.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.477
- [Kelly] Mm hmm.

00:06:32.477 --> 00:06:35.680
- And so, because I knew
that's what was expected,

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.760
I kind of took this detour around it.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.370
And rather than write something specific,

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:43.880
I wrote about the core of
it was much more emotional

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:44.713
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:44.713 --> 00:06:46.130
- in general so that people can relate.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:47.870
- That's cool though that is
kind of therapeutic for you

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.870
though as well while
doing it. That's cool.

